ed in Houston, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Ezra Y. Yacob | CEO & Chairman | Age 48 | Compensation: $4.8M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 3K people. Revenue per employee of $6.7M ranks in the top quartile among Energy peers, improving at 10%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $2.8M of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $64K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["EOG Resources, Inc. is a large-cap energy business with a $77.2B market cap on $22.6B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -4% over the past three years and +10% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 23% against a 8% cost of capital \u2014 a 15-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $9.6B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 42 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.02\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","Accruals are elevated at -9.8% of assets, and Sloan showed firms building earnings through accruals rather than cash systematically underperform.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.4\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 27\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Management returns a meaningful 6.1% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 2.6\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $7.8M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Financial quality metrics lag Energy peers","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Energy tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"The commodity cycle \u2014 revenue and free cash flow are ultimately hostage to a price the company does not set, and the long-run energy-transition arc adds a structural overhang.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Geopolitical supply shocks \u2014 wars, sanctions, and OPEC decisions create volatility that no hedging programme can fully neutralise.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 13% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Trading at a 52% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Valued at a 31% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA","36 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","ROIC sits in the top quartile of Energy peers \u2014 the moat is wider than the neighbourhood","Energy sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 